# Written per worktree by scripts/setup-ocaml-env.sh, never committed: dune roots
# at the OUTERMOST ancestor holding a dune-workspace, so a tracked copy at the repo
# root would shadow the one each nested .claude/worktrees/ checkout needs and pin
# every worktree's builds back to the main checkout.
dune-workspace

# Local OPAM switch
_opam/
.vscode/settings.json

# Local configuration: only the repository-root one is personal. Nested ocannl_config
# files are test/benchmark fixtures and must be committed, so the pattern is anchored
# rather than matching at every depth (which needed a negation line per fixture, and
# silently green suites whenever one was forgotten).
/ocannl_config
# Convenience for storing particular configurations, not used by OCANNL
ocannl_config-*

# Debug output
log_files/
build_files/

# Autotune schedule caches: the built-in default (autotune_cache_dir) and the per-test
# directories named at the tests' Autotune.tune ~cache_dir call sites. They appear here
# only when a test exe is run from the repo root by hand; under dune runtest the cwd is
# inside _build/, which is why a missing entry used to stay invisible for months.
# One glob instead of a hand-maintained list: test/operations/cache_dir_ignores requires
# every argument that names a cache directory -- Autotune.tune's ~cache_dir and a direct
# Schedule_cache operation's ~dir -- to be a single name with this prefix, so it cannot
# fall behind.
# Root-anchored deliberately: a cache directory is only ever created in the working
# directory, and an unanchored pattern would hide a stray copy anywhere in the tree.
/autotune_cache*/

# Staging files of Utils.Atomic_file: a publication that was interrupted between staging and
# rename, anywhere a checkpoint or cache is written into the tree. Unanchored, because the
# artifact sits beside whatever file was being published. The helper's own sweep removes them
# once they are stale; this keeps one from reaching a commit in the meantime.
#
# Spelled to the GENERATED shape and nothing wider: `*.ocannl-stage.*` would also hide a file
# somebody named `report.ocannl-stage.backup` from `git status` and so from their own commit, and
# `[0-9]*` is no better -- git reads that as one digit followed by anything, so `1abc` matches too.
# A glob has no way to say "one or more digits", so the staging name's fields are fixed-width hex
# (8, 8, 16) precisely so this rule can be EXACT over them. The leading `?*` is one-or-more: a bare
# `*` also matches the EMPTY stem, and `.ocannl-stage.<fields>` is a name the helper never generates
# and so somebody else's file. test/operations/atomic_file_race derives this whole line from
# atomic_file.ml's infix and field widths, then checks it against the recognizer.
#
# One dimension this rule CANNOT reach, and it is the language's limit rather than an oversight: a
# generated stem is at most 191 bytes (longer basenames are truncated and digested), and a glob has
# no way to bound a length -- `?`-repetition would spell it in 192 characters. So a file with a stem
# longer than that AND this exact 34-character suffix is hidden here while `is_staging_file` rejects
# it. Nothing DELETES such a file: the recognizer is what the sweep consults, and it is exact. The
# residue is a name no person plausibly writes being absent from `git status` (Codex P2, round 8).
?*.ocannl-stage.[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f].[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]

# Test artifacts written to the working directory
